# Discord:
1. Go to https://discord.com/developers/applications
2. Create a new application
3. On the left navigation, click "Bot"
4. Find "Token", then click reset token
5. Follow though and then copy the token
6. Save the token


# Code:
1. Fork the project
2. Create a venv, installing requirements.txt

       pip install -r requirements.txt
   
4. Create a .env

    a. add in DISCORD_TOKEN=discord_token
   
    b. discord_token should be the Token saved prior when creating the bot in Discord Developer.


# OR, USE DOCKER! (you may still need to setup the Discord bot, if one isn't already made):

1. Make a new folder
```mkdir discord-llm-bot```

Ex. where I called the folder 'docker-test' instead:

![create folder](readme-images/create-folder.png)

2. Enter into the folder
```cd discord-llm-bot```

3. Copy in the .env file and the data folder.

![copy in the data](readme-images/copy_in_data.png)

Or, make your own.

For env, it needs:
 the API key for the model you are using (if any)

 the discord bot token (see the full setup guide for how to get this)

 the channel id of the forum you want the bot to be able to respond in.


### .env
```DISCORD_TOKEN={token from creating the bot}```
```GEMINI_API_KEY={api key from gemini}```
```# SPECIFIC forum channel that the chatbot listens to```
```ALLOWED_FORUM_ID={specific channel id for the bot to respond in}```


### ./data
Copy the file structure seen in the image. The server_code is is the unique Discord server number, accessible through developer mode. You just need to copy it from Discord and paste it in.

Currently, the text files are exactly that - text files. You can put in whatever information you want. Just make sure the name of the text files matches the discord tag. If you make other subfolders asside from "projects" and "concepts" make sure to add it to:

- services/class_context.py
- commands/chatbot.py - find_context_file()


For the {server_code}.json file, is should be structured like:
```
{
  "class_name": "CS101 - Introduction to Computer Science",
  "professor": "Dr. Jane Smith",
  "office_hours": "Mon & Wed, 2:00-4:00 PM (Room 314)",
  "class_times": "Tue & Thu, 10:30-11:45 AM",
  "location": "Lecture Hall A",
  "contact": "smith@example.edu",
  "syllabus": "https://example.edu/cs101/syllabus"
}
```

Then, the code expects students.xlsx to be structured like:
email, username, userid, fullname, lastname, firstname


It expects those 6 categories. Insert the appropriate information.

4. Open Docker on your computer. If you don't have it instealled, please go and install the app. https://www.docker.com/get-started/ 

![alt text](readme-images/open_docker_step3.png)


4. Still inside discord-llm-bot folder:

```docker build -t cs-bot https://github.com/heb229/nau-discord-bot.git```

5. Then, run it.

Unix:
```docker run --env-file .env -v $(pwd)/data:/app/data cs-bot```

Windows:
```docker run --env-file .env -v ${PWD}/data:/app/data cs-bot```


Optional (if you want it running in the background):

Start it in the background:

```docker run -d --env-file .env -v $(pwd)/data:/app/data --name cs-bot-container cs-bot```

Check the logs:

```docker logs -f cs-bot-container```

Stop the run (disable to bot):

```docker stop cs-bot-container```

It also doesn't hurt to reopen the Docker app, click on 'Containers' and just make sure the container isn't running anymore either.
